Z||000002||william_thorniii||03-28-2000||07:58 PM||||lord,<P>Bridging is the combination of 2 or more channels of an amplifiers output.  So if you have a 75W through 4 channels, you can put two channels together effectively making your amplifier a 3 channel amp.  A good thing to note is that the wattage is not added linearly.  You actually get closer to 200 W from this configuration than the expected 150W.<P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||03-28-2000||08:36 PM||||A little less in depth, Bridging is when you take a - and a + from 2 channels to make it one.  I have my 10" sub bridged on a triax amp making it run at 400 watts peak, instead of like 100 for 1 channel.  and I can still put 2 more speakers on the circuit if I can get the right ohm levels  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||overlord||03-28-2000||08:59 PM||||ahh...k thanx guys..i thought it was somehting like that..one more question..<P>does the sound quality change at all when u bridge??||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-28-2000||10:29 PM||||If you bridge a two channel amp, you lose stereo, but that don't matter to a sub.||||reg||1
Z||000006||zebrex||03-29-2000||12:05 AM||||you guys are forgeting something if he has two subs and bridges them he will cut the ohm load in half so the amp needs too be two ohm stable bridged or it will smoke in a hurry (or use 8ohm subs)||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||03-29-2000||01:10 AM||||   When you bridge 2 channels, the Total Harmonic Distortion of those 2 channels is added together.  As long as this stays below 3-5% you wont notice with a sub.  Happy pounding!!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Racer 1||03-29-2000||01:54 AM||||Make sure you get a high quality amp like Rockford Fosgate,Kicker or PPI. If you go with a low quality amp when you turn it up it get distorted. I have been hooking up amps and systems for about 8 years now. ||||reg||5
Z||000009||overlord||03-29-2000||10:38 AM||||ok..im just going with a cheap Kenwood amp..guess i wont bother bridging...someone metnioned the word "smoking".-)||||reg||1
Z||000010||olympic||03-29-2000||04:31 PM||||   If an amp is stable down to 2 ohms in stereo(99% of amps are), it can handle a 4 ohm mono load.  If its stable to 1 ohm in stereo, it can take a 2 ohm mono load, and so on.<P>     Bridgig your Kenwood amp(even if it is a cheaper model) will be completely safe as long as the load is 3-4 ohms nominal.  This means running a single 4 ohm sub or if you run two subs, they will have to be dual voice coil models or some of the new ones that come with 2 ohm voice coils(wire these subs in series), 6 ohm(wire these in parallel), and 8 ohm(parallel).  <P>     If you already bought two 4 ohm single voice coil subs, you can "try" and wire them in parallel and run them in mono from your amp.  The amp may: 1)not turn on at all 2)get hot quickly and shut down 3)get fairly warm but work fine.   Remember, the voice coil resistance varies with frequency.  The 4 ohm rating is usually the lowest resistance the sub will produce.  When you are playing music, the actual resistance will be much higer(10-30 ohms per sub)  Don't worry about burning it out because modern amps have all kinds of protection built in.  It will shut down before damage occurs.<P>     If your amp refuses to run a 2 ohm mono load, your stuck running your subs in stereo.<P><BR>     <P>     ||||reg||1
Z||000011||stimpy||03-29-2000||09:52 PM||||Bridging... could you dumb it down more for me? Do I hook up only the R+ and L- or what?  I just picked up a SAS Bazooka 6.5" and a Phase Linear amp that says it bridges to 150w. It gets mounted to the bottom of the dash on the passenger side.Should go well with the Bass Shakers! Pray for good Fiero-workin-on-weather for this weekend!  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||DJRice||03-29-2000||11:00 PM||||The amps dont work once you let the smoke out.....Just a tip||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||03-29-2000||11:18 PM||||Wired in parallel(both speakers pos wires together,both speakers neg wires together)will add the two speaker's ohms together to a larger impedance.<BR>Wired together in series(one speaker's neg going into the other speaker's pos.)will give you half of the two speakers ohms average.Example; two 4ohm speakers together in series will give 2ohms load for the amp.<BR>So if your subs are 8ohm speakers,then you may wind up with a 4ohm combo.<BR>My home speakers are(each channel)two 4ohm 8",in series(2ohms there)with a 4ohm,mid/tweeter in parallel with that,all together giving around 6ohms.<BR>And,Stimpy,yeah,that's how the ones that do work in mono say to do it.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||olympic||03-30-2000||02:37 AM||||lowCG,<BR>   You've got that backwards.  Wiring speakers in series adds the impedance of the speakers together.  Paralleling two speakers of equal impedance cuts the final impedance in half.<P>Stimpy,<BR>    Your amp should be marked which 2 of the 4 outputs(L+,L-,R+,R-) you use for bridging. There sould be a line drawn between two terminals with "bridged" or "mono" marked on it.  If there is nothing there check the manual. ||||reg||4
Z||000015||fierodave||03-30-2000||04:05 AM||||Hmmmmmmm  and to think I thought it was the process of joining two land masses together ;~)||||reg||1
Z||000016||Racer 1||03-30-2000||01:46 PM||||I also have a cheap KAC-606 Kenwood amp in my metro. The bad thing is if you run the kenwood amp bridged it will heat up real fast then cut out till the temp comes down. My Kenwood amp barely powered a JL Audio 10" in my metro. ||||reg||9
